────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PARITY DISPOSITION
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
The web `Layout` is the application chrome. Its UNIQUE responsibility — distinct from the banner/modal
sub-components it merely MOUNTS, which the prompt scopes Out ("Other surfaces in the same feature each
have their own prompt": OfflineBanner, MaintenanceBanner, SessionExpiringModal, TourOverlay,
NotificationBellPopover, ThemePicker, StatusBar, CommandPalette, …) — is the NAVIGATION SHELL:

  • The full `navSections` catalog (19 sections, 100 destinations) ported verbatim to Layout.Nav.swift,
    each web `Icons.*` glyph mapped to its nearest SF Symbol; `minVehicles` / `requiresAuth` carried through.
  • The grouped sidebar: active-"Current" card with the Pin/Pinned toggle, the Pinned group (cap 8) with
    per-row unpin, the Recently-Used group (feature-flagged off, web `SHOW_RECENTLY_USED_NAV=false`), and
    the collapsible Sections group with Expand-all / Collapse-all.
  • Per-item count badges: unread alerts on /notifications/alerts (capped 9+), the uncapped fleet size on
    /vehicles, the stale-session count on /data-repair (capped 9+) — verbatim from the web `renderNavLink`.
  • Visibility filter (`isVisibleNavItem`), active-path match (`isActiveNavPath` — `/` exact vs prefix),
    pin/unpin/recordVisit mutators, section-toggle (active section never collapses), active-section
    auto-expand — all ported 1:1 into the pure `LayoutProjector` and unit-pinned.
  • Header chrome: brand lockup (shared `TSLogo`), the quick theme switcher (web local `ThemeQuickSwitcher`
    → trigger `theme.openPicker` + "Customize…" `theme.customize`), the notification-bell trigger
    (`NotificationBellPopover` placement, with the unread chip), the compact-width sidebar open/close
    (`nav.openSidebar` / `nav.closeSidebar`).
  • Content region: web `<main role="main">` with the breadcrumb-row quick-search hint
    (`nav.quickSearchHint`) over the routed `<Outlet>` slot (host-supplied `content`).
  • Adaptive: permanent sidebar column at regular width (iPad/macOS HIG); drawer behind the header bar at
    compact width (iPhone HIG).

Data binding (P1/S8): the shell's live inputs — sidebar style (`useSidebarStyle`), route (`useLocation`),
fleet/alert/stale counts (`useQuery`), auth mode (`useIsForwardAuth`/`useAuthMode`) — arrive via the
`LayoutSource` snapshot; navigation routes back out through the host `onSelect` (the native peer of
`<NavLink to>` / `useNavigate`). `useTranslation` → the P1/S10 facade (LayoutStrings). The web's
side-effect-only hooks (useToast, useRealtimeEvents, useNotificationListener, useDynamicAppIcon,
useTitleBadge, useFaviconBadge, useCriticalAlertFlash, useKeyboardShortcuts, useStatusBarPrefs, useTour,
useAnnouncer, useGlobalProgress) are app-global subsystems the shell MOUNTS; they are owned by their own
P4 surfaces/services and are not navigation-render data. No HTTP lives in the view. Charts/maps: NONE in
source → NONE here.

NOTE ON TYPE NAME: the public Swift type is `LayoutShell` (not `Layout`) — the bare name `Layout` collides
with the SwiftUI `Layout` protocol and breaks expression-context construction in-module (proven: see gate
[7]). The surface slug, the file names (Layout.*), and every i18n key remain "Layout". Composition view
`LayoutShellFreshnessChip` is likewise prefixed to avoid the pre-existing `LayoutFreshnessChip` enum in the
sibling `LayoutSwitcher` surface (collision found + avoided before any build — gate [8]).

